  "title": "Prayer at Masjid Buratha — Baghdad",
  "data": "Visit Masjid Buratha, on the pilgrims' road between Kazimayn and Baghdad, and pray and supplicate in it: Imam Ali (a.s.) prayed here with his sons Imam al-Hasan (a.s.) and Imam al-Husayn (a.s.).",
  "merits": "Masjid Buratha is a famous and blessed mosque on the pilgrims' road between Baghdad and Kazimayn, yet most pilgrims miss its blessing, though many merits and distinctions of it are reported.\n\nYaqut al-Hamawi, a historian of the seventh century AH, writes in Mu'jam al-Buldan that Buratha was a quarter of Baghdad south of Bab Muhawwal, on the qiblah side of al-Karkh, with a mosque where the Shia offered the five daily prayers. Before the reign of the Abbasid caliph al-Radi billah the Shia used to gather there, until al-Radi's soldiers raided the mosque, imprisoned those they found in it and razed it to the ground. The Shia told Bajkam al-Makani, the commander in Baghdad, and he ordered it rebuilt, larger and stronger, with al-Radi's name inscribed over its front. It stayed in use, with congregational prayers, until 450 AH, and was deserted after that. It has since been rebuilt and is again a place of prayer.\n\nBefore Baghdad was built, Buratha was a village. Imam Ali (a.s.) is said to have passed through it on his way to fight the Khawarij at al-Nahrawan, prayed in this mosque and bathed in the village's bathhouse. The place is named after Abu Shu'ayb al-Burathi, a worshipper and ascetic who was the first to settle at Buratha, in a hut where he worshipped until the end of his life. The daughter of a wealthy family, raised in palaces, passed his hut, was drawn to his way of life and asked to serve him. He accepted her on condition that she give up her finery; she took the dress of worshippers, and he married her. When she entered the hut she saw a reed mat he had laid down against the damp, and said she would not stay unless he threw it out, for she had heard him say that the earth tells the son of Adam: \"You put a screen between me and you, yet tomorrow you will be in my belly.\" Abu Shu'ayb threw the mat out, and the two lived together in the best of worship until they died.\n\nShaykh Abbas al-Qummi writes that in Hadiyyat al-Za'ir he gathered the narrations on this mosque, from which it has merits any one of which would make a mosque worth travelling far to pray and supplicate in: Allah has decreed that no king with his army shall camp on this ground except a prophet or a prophet's successor; the house of Lady Maryam (a.s.) stood here; it is the land of Prophet Isa (a.s.); here the spring appeared for Lady Maryam (a.s.), and Imam Ali (a.s.) brought it out again; here is a blessed white rock on which Lady Maryam (a.s.) laid Prophet Isa (a.s.), which Imam Ali (a.s.) brought out again by a miracle, set up facing the qiblah and prayed towards; Imam Ali (a.s.) and his sons Imam al-Hasan (a.s.) and Imam al-Husayn (a.s.) prayed in this mosque; Imam Ali (a.s.) stayed here four days for the holiness of the place; the prophets prayed here, Prophet Ibrahim (a.s.) above all; a prophet is buried here, perhaps Prophet Yusha (a.s.), for the Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) says his grave is outside Kazimayn, opposite Buratha; and here the sun was turned back for Imam Ali (a.s.).\n\nHe adds that, for all these merits and all the signs and miracles of Imam Ali (a.s.) shown here, hardly one pilgrim in a thousand goes into the mosque, though it is on their road and they pass it twice, coming and going; and that one who finds its door shut will not spend a little to have it opened, while spending freely on sightseeing in Baghdad and on needless shopping."
}
